The verified monitor drops the events of predicates its formula does not mention
before converting them, as MonPoly does (Filter_rel).  The log has events of
predicates each formula ignores, time points that carry only such events (which
must still count as time points for PREV, NEXT, the windows and MATCHP), and
events of P where LET binds P (which the formula must not see).  The expected
output was recorded from the verified monitor before the filter and agrees with
MonPoly's.

  $ while IFS= read -r phi; do
  >   [ -z "$phi" ] && continue
  >   printf '%s\n' "$phi" > f.mfotl
  >   echo "=== $phi"
  >   verimon -sig filt.sig -formula f.mfotl -log filt.log 2>&1
  > done < formulas.txt
  === P(x)
  @0 (time point 0): (1)
  @3 (time point 3): (2) (3)
  @6 (time point 6): (4)
  @10 (time point 8): (1)
  === PREV[0,2] P(x)
  @1 (time point 1): (1)
  @5 (time point 4): (2) (3)
  === ONCE[0,3] P(x)
  @0 (time point 0): (1)
  @1 (time point 1): (1)
  @2 (time point 2): (1)
  @3 (time point 3): (1) (2) (3)
  @5 (time point 4): (2) (3)
  @6 (time point 5): (2) (3)
  @6 (time point 6): (2) (3) (4)
  @9 (time point 7): (4)
  @10 (time point 8): (1)
  === P(x) SINCE[0,5] Q(x)
  @0 (time point 0): (2)
  @1 (time point 1): (3)
  @3 (time point 3): (1)
  @5 (time point 4): (3)
  @6 (time point 5): (2)
  @6 (time point 6): (4)
  === NEXT[0,3] P(x)
  @2 (time point 2): (2) (3)
  @6 (time point 5): (4)
  @9 (time point 7): (1)
  === EVENTUALLY[0,4] P(x)
  @0 (time point 0): (1) (2) (3)
  @1 (time point 1): (2) (3)
  @2 (time point 2): (2) (3) (4)
  @3 (time point 3): (2) (3) (4)
  @5 (time point 4): (4)
  @6 (time point 5): (1) (4)
  @6 (time point 6): (1) (4)
  @9 (time point 7): (1)
  @10 (time point 8): (1)
  === r <- CNT x ONCE[0,4] P(x)
  @0 (time point 0): (1)
  @1 (time point 1): (1)
  @2 (time point 2): (1)
  @3 (time point 3): (3)
  @5 (time point 4): (2)
  @6 (time point 5): (2)
  @6 (time point 6): (3)
  @9 (time point 7): (1)
  @10 (time point 8): (2)
  === LET P(x) = Q(x) IN P(x)
  @0 (time point 0): (2)
  @1 (time point 1): (3)
  @3 (time point 3): (1)
  @5 (time point 4): (3)
  @6 (time point 5): (2)
  @6 (time point 6): (4)
  === LET P(x) = Q(x) IN ONCE[0,3] P(x)
  @0 (time point 0): (2)
  @1 (time point 1): (2) (3)
  @2 (time point 2): (2) (3)
  @3 (time point 3): (1) (2) (3)
  @5 (time point 4): (1) (3)
  @6 (time point 5): (1) (2) (3)
  @6 (time point 6): (1) (2) (3) (4)
  @9 (time point 7): (2) (4)
  === (LET P(x) = Q(x) IN P(x)) AND ONCE[0,10] R(x,y)
  @1 (time point 1): (3,4)
  @3 (time point 3): (1,2)
  @5 (time point 4): (3,4)
  @6 (time point 5): (2,2)
  @6 (time point 6): (4,1)
  === LET Q(x) = EXISTS y. R(x,y) IN P(x) AND NOT Q(x)
  @3 (time point 3): (2) (3)
  === MATCHP[0,4] (P(x)? . Q(x)?)
  @5 (time point 4): (3)

The filter is switched off with -nofilterrel, as for MonPoly, with the same result.

  $ echo 'LET P(x) = Q(x) IN ONCE[0,3] P(x)' > g.mfotl
  $ verimon -nofilterrel -sig filt.sig -formula g.mfotl -log filt.log
  @0 (time point 0): (2)
  @1 (time point 1): (2) (3)
  @2 (time point 2): (2) (3)
  @3 (time point 3): (1) (2) (3)
  @5 (time point 4): (1) (3)
  @6 (time point 5): (1) (2) (3)
  @6 (time point 6): (1) (2) (3) (4)
  @9 (time point 7): (2) (4)
